---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Pending from "../components/Pending.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The Naha"
  description="A short history and orientation for the Naha — the chain of lakes running west from Heckman to saltwater at Loring, and the valley the proposed Shelter Cove connector would reach."
  canonical="/naha"
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-4 py-12">
    <p class="font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-tide">Background</p>
    <h1 class="font-display mt-2 text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight">The Naha</h1>
    <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink/90">
      A short history and orientation for the valley at the north end of the island — the
      country the proposed
      <a href="/projects/naha-connector" class="underline decoration-flagging underline-offset-4 hover:text-tide">
        Shelter Cove connector</a> would reach.
    </p>

    <div class="prose-revilla mt-10 space-y-6 leading-relaxed">
      <section>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold">Land of the distant lakes</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          Naha is a Tlingit name, and the river runs west through a chain of lakes for
          seventeen miles to saltwater. The mouth was an ancient Tlingit village where, by
          oral tradition, the Eagle clan lived on one bank and the Wolf clan on the other.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold">The cannery years</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          A saltery went in at Naha Bay in 1883, a cannery in 1888. By 1897 Loring packed more
          salmon than any cannery in Alaska, and it held the only post office in southern
          Southeast before Ketchikan grew. In 1901 the operation built the Fortmann Hatchery on
          Heckman Lake and connected it to tidewater with a tramway along the river. The runs
          declined after 1913 and the cannery came apart around 1930.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold">What's left</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          A few families at Loring. Four Forest Service cabins. A
          <a href="/network/naha-river" class="underline decoration-contour underline-offset-4 hover:text-tide">National Recreation Trail</a>.
          Steelhead. And a valley that has been industrially connected to the coast before, and
          isn't now.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-10 border border-contour/70 bg-sheet px-4 py-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-tide">
      <Pending>[To add]</Pending> what the association's involvement here has been, and any oral
      history or photographs gathered locally. A page like this is also where a Loring resident
      or a Tlingit tribal voice belongs, if they're willing.
    </p>

    <p class="mt-8 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-tide">
      Read next ·{" "}
      <a href="/projects/naha-connector" class="underline decoration-contour underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink">Shelter Cove to Loring</a>{" "}
      ·{" "}
      <a href="/network/naha-river" class="underline decoration-contour underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink">Naha River trail page</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .prose-revilla :global(a) {
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 4px;
  }
  .prose-revilla :global(em) {
    font-family: var(--font-hydro);
    font-style: italic;
  }
</style>
